#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Diagnostic tool for async question queues (epic 23).

Usage:
    claude-questions [--workspace-dir DIR] [--check] [--check-contract [DIR]]
                     [--reindex] [--status] [--json]

Offline by default: reads the workspace ``[questions]`` config, the listener
index at ``~/.claude/async_questions.json``, and the listener status file.
Never prints token material.

--check
    Probe each distinct installation token against the relay (network required).
    Reports bound / not-bound / invalid for each.  Exit 2 on any problem.
    Mirrors ``claude-roles --check``.

--check-contract [DIR]
    Report every heading that does **not** conform to the parse contract
    (architecture §3.2) and what to do about it.  Never modifies any file.
    Exit 2 if categories 1-3 (malformed id, missing status, duplicate id) are
    found in any file.  Intended for workspace adopters.

--reindex
    Scan every queue file for ``**Dispatched:** … relay #N`` markers and
    rebuild missing index entries (message_id → workspace/qid mapping).
    Never touches a queue file; never overwrites an existing index record.
    Use this after an index write fails (``index_routing_failed: True`` in the
    ``ask`` return value) — the listener will then pick up the pending answer.

--status
    Print detailed listener state: connection state, watermarks, and pending
    applies.  Does not require the listener lock.  This is the first thing to
    read when "a question was answered and nothing happened".

Exit codes:
    0  clean — including "no [questions] configured" (that is valid, not a problem)
    1  config or catalog errors
    2  (--check) any token unbound or invalid
    2  (--check-contract) non-conforming entries found
"""

